{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"The claim is too broad to assess without specifying a country, year, and comparison population.

“Overrepresented” means a group’s share of prisoners exceeds its share of the relevant general population. Being the largest racial group in prison does not, by itself, establish overrepresentation.

In the United States, white people are generally **underrepresented** in prison relative to their share of the population, particularly when comparing non-Hispanic white people consistently across both datasets. Results elsewhere depend on local demographics and how race is recorded.

So, as a universal claim, it is unsupported; as a claim about the United States, it is generally false."}}
